<%# locals: (key:, name:, label_id:) %>
<%#
  PINNING IS A CONTROL YOU CAN SEE (2026-11-02, UX audit G2). A right click is invisible until
  someone tries it, and iOS has none; this posts to the same door the right-click menu does
  (Tools::PinsController), and its redirect back is what redraws the rail.

  A TOGGLE, WHOSE TWO NAMES AGREE WITH ITS STATE. `aria-pressed` carries the state to assistive
  tech and the word carries it on screen ("Pinned to sidebar"; never the ink fill alone,
  design-system.md rule 13). The words never flip to the opposite action: "Unpin, pressed" reads as
  either state, which is why the house toggles keep a name that describes the thing pressed. The
  title says what a press will do. `aria-describedby` names the tool, because every card's button
  has the same words. Hidden below 720px, where there is no sidebar to pin to (pages/tools.css).
%>
<% pinned = current_user.tool_pins.pinned?(key) %>
<%= button_to (pinned ? "Pinned to sidebar" : "Pin to sidebar"), tools_pin_path(key),
              method: pinned ? :delete : :post, class: "btn small tool-pin", form_class: "tool-pin-form",
              aria: { pressed: pinned, describedby: label_id },
              title: pinned ? "Unpin #{name} from the sidebar" : "Pin #{name} to the sidebar" %>
